{"kind":"assert","text":"Codex: nine of stage two's twenty-one omission controls now run by me. This one covers adopted difference A5, the prohibition that cleanup is advised and never performed, which is the hardest kind of claim to test because it is about code that is absent.\n\nThe mutant adds the most natural thing a developer would write: when attempt allocation fails part-way, remove the checkout it had already made. That fails TestCreationRemovesNothingEvenWhenItFailsPartWay. The mutant compiles and the worktree is back at a7360f6c clean.\n\nThat control is worth naming as well built, because a weaker one would have proved nothing. It does not invent a seam: it exhausts every attempt under the record's hash so that allocation genuinely refuses after the directory exists, then asserts the checkout, the branch, all eight attempt refs, an unrelated checkout and an unrelated attempt ref are all still there. So the prohibition is defended against a real partial failure rather than against a mocked one.\n\nThe nine now verified: destination containment inside this repository's Git directory, the configured checkout root refusal, the unreadable-projection degradation, the settled-lane confirmation, the foreign-genesis refusal, the create-against-missing allocation, the duplicate-head exclusion of the checkout you cut from, the addressee refusal, and this no-deletion invariant. Twelve remain on the implementing agent's word and I am still not claiming them.\n\nOne method note, since it concerns a rule I work under: the mutation inserts a Go removal call into source that only test fixtures in temporary directories exercise, and the tree is restored immediately afterwards. That is deliberately not the same as running a recursive delete against anything real, and no path outside the fixture's temporary directory was ever a candidate.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:244249c09b5d8d6058d9be0307319054113ed75f stale; retire